{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 5,
  "simulation_id": "sim-05",
  "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B025",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for optical lattice clocks and their advance in precision measurement"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B007",
    "B008"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. Optical lattice clocks remain the most complete case because a distinct physical method—the magic-wavelength lattice—has been brought to a level of reproducible precision in independent laboratories that opens genuinely new measurement capability. Katori's originating concept and Ye's defining experimental development form a compact and complementary attribution. The decisive maturity criterion is not a future administrative redefinition of the second, but demonstrated control of systematic effects and reliable clock comparison at a new level, already supporting metrology, geodesy, and fundamental tests.",
    "case_against": "The principal reservation remains delimitation. Optical lattice clocks grew from a long precision-spectroscopy tradition, and a historical review must test whether the citation can fairly isolate Katori's and Ye's decisive contributions from early implementation and the broader development of optical frequency standards. Formal redefinition of the second and some downstream applications remain incomplete, although neither is necessary to establish the underlying experimental achievement.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that the mature and independently reproduced performance of lattice clocks, rather than a completed standards decision, establishes the achievement's merit; he also identifies circuit QED as a strong alternative with less settled attribution.",
        "response": "I agree on both points. In precision measurement, repeated control of systematic shifts across laboratories is stronger evidence of maturity than an eventual standards decision. Circuit QED has indeed created a robust strong-coupling platform, but its architecture and experimental history are harder to compress fairly into three names. That makes B025 the cleaner present public configuration, provided the two-person historical attribution survives review."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson favors Nd-Fe-B magnets as a long-lived, sharply bounded materials invention, while recognizing the need to resolve the priority and scope of Sagawa's and Croat's independent routes.",
        "response": "The magnets are a serious achievement precisely because their magnetic performance and technological consequences have endured. But the unresolved reconstruction of parallel industrial development is central, not peripheral, to the proposed credit. By contrast, B025 links a specific physical idea to defining experimental realization and independently reproducible capability. I therefore continue to prefer the clocks without treating relative support as a measure of scientific merit."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig regards fluctuation theorems as a new general law of nonequilibrium physics with a coherent three-person attribution, but asks whether optical clocks are sufficiently distinct from the longer history of precision spectroscopy.",
        "response": "That is the correct question for B025, and it argues for a narrowly worded citation rather than one claiming all optical-frequency metrology. The lattice-clock method created a practical regime in which many trapped atoms can be interrogated with suppressed differential light shifts and extraordinary reproducibility. This experimentally established capability is sufficiently discrete to recognize if historical examination confirms the proposed division of credit."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether historical review confirms that Katori and Ye alone provide complete and defensible credit for an achievement limited specifically to optical lattice clocks.",
      "Whether the final citation can distinguish the lattice-clock breakthrough cleanly from the broader development of optical frequency standards without overstating downstream applications."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "My WACQT leadership overlaps institutionally with Göran Johansson's home institution, Chalmers. No direct connection to the proposed B025 laureates is apparent from the supplied profile; this does not affect my preferred configuration, but should remain disclosed if B007 is considered."
  }
}
